t lifecycle, snapshot persistence ordering, log compaction, retention, and recovery boundaries."},{"file":"configuration.go","start_line":44,"end_line":372,"reason":"Resolve membership representation, quorum-relevant suffrage, configuration validation and transitions, persistence encoding, and prevIndex safeguards."},{"file":"config.go","start_line":99,"end_line":376,"reason":"Resolve protocol and snapshot version choices, timeout and reloadable configuration, validation constraints, and enabled-versus-used mechanisms."}],"rationale":"The catalogue supports a broad implementation-grounded survey across decision progression, role and timeout control, durable history and recovery, membership, transport-facing replication, and decision-to-service effects. The selected ranges prioritize concrete producers, consumers, shared state, and cross-thread handoffs while separating implementation observations from documentation expectations. Existing initial materials describe the intended apply sequence and high-level roles, but do not establish the actual behavior of the core loops, persistence paths, membership transitions, or asynchronous response handling.","related_ids":["docs/apply.md:1:100","docs/README.md:1:100","README.md:1:100"],"gap":"The remaining unexplored areas include exact transport framing and pipeline failure behavior, concrete LogStore and StableStore error semantics, FileSnapshotStore atomicity and reclamation details, public API request admission and future timeout behavior, and the correspondence between existing tests and the recovered implementation. No goal or obligation is selected until these implementation paths and applicable contracts are reviewed."}
